@* For employers. The call to action preselects the contact topic. *@
@inject IContactTopicState TopicState
<section class="section" id="services" aria-labelledby="services-title">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">02 / For Employers</p>
    <h2 id="services-title">What I Offer Employers</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">For companies hiring a senior full-stack engineer. These are the six areas I own on a product team, from the first design review through production support, and the standard I hold each one to.</p>
    <div class="card-grid">
      <article class="card service-card">
        <div class="service-icon" aria-hidden="true">
          <Icon Name="design" />
        </div>
        <h3>Design</h3>
        <p>Turning requirements from Product into scalable design blueprints. I research the problem space and the systems already in play, then break the work into epics, stories, and tasks with clear acceptance criteria.</p>
        <p>From there I plan Sprint 0, lead the architecture review, and guide the development effort through delivery, documenting progress, decisions, and challenges so the whole team can see where things stand.</p>
        <p>An engineered design is the keystone.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="card service-card">
        <div class="service-icon" aria-hidden="true">
          <Icon Name="data" />
        </div>
        <h3>Data</h3>
        <p>Schema design and T-SQL on SQL Server: stored procedures, indexing strategy, and execution plan analysis for queries that stay fast under load. On AWS, single-table DynamoDB models with keys and access patterns designed up front.</p>
        <p>Data stays tight from the database to the browser: typed models in the API, versioned request and response contracts, and TypeScript interfaces that mirror them, so a change in one layer is caught at compile time rather than in production.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="card service-card">
        <div class="service-icon" aria-hidden="true">
          <Icon Name="code" />
        </div>
        <h3>Code</h3>
        <p>Maintainable, efficient C# and TypeScript written to conventions refined over 15 years, built on SOLID principles and modern software engineering patterns, readable by the next engineer without a tour, and kept that way through analyzers, code review, and small, focused pull requests.</p>
        <p>The same standard governs my AI tooling: enterprise-grade Claude agents equipped with skills for the healthcare industry, including HL7 v2, FHIR, and HIPAA, and for the accounting domain, grounded in what production code and code-review feedback taught me so generated code meets the team's conventions before a human reads it.</p>
        <p>One standard for every line, by me or by machine: it's the same engineering.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="card service-card">
        <div class="service-icon" aria-hidden="true">
          <Icon Name="test" />
        </div>
        <h3>Test</h3>
        <p>Every feature ships with fully functional unit and integration tests in xUnit or NUnit, with Moq for mocking, covering the happy path, edge cases, and failure modes.</p>
        <p>Tests run in the pipeline on every push, so refactoring stays safe and regressions surface before review rather than in production.</p>
        <p>Tests are the guardrails.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="card service-card">
        <div class="service-icon" aria-hidden="true">
          <Icon Name="deploy" />
        </div>
        <h3>Deploy</h3>
        <p>CI/CD pipelines in Azure DevOps or GitHub Actions that build, test, and ship Docker images, from GitOps deployments with ArgoCD on Kubernetes down to lean deploy scripts for small serverless projects on AWS.</p>
        <p>Environments are reproducible, rollbacks are a single step, and every release leaves a trail from commit to running service.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="card service-card">
        <div class="service-icon" aria-hidden="true">
          <Icon Name="monitor" />
        </div>
        <h3>Monitor &amp; Support</h3>
        <p>Production is instrumented with structured logging through Serilog, shipped to Seq, Datadog, or CloudWatch, with dashboards and alarms tuned so problems surface before customers notice.</p>
        <p>When something does slip through, I switch on debug-level logging for the affected component, trace the request through the logs, run the root-cause analysis, and ship the fix with a test that keeps it from coming back.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <div class="button-row section-cta">
      <a class="button button-primary" href="#contact" @onclick="@(() => TopicState.Select("Job opportunity"))">Talk about a role</a>
      <a class="button button-secondary" href="#resume">See my experience</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
